'''antitypal''' (an′ti-tī-pal), ''a.'' [< ''antitype'' + ''-al.''] Relating to or of the nature of an antitype.
{{smaller block|How am I to extricate my ''antitypal'' characters, when their living types have not yet extricated themselves?
{{right|''Kingsley'', Yeast, Epil.}}
We still see remaining an ''antitypal'' sketch of a wing adapted for flight in the scaly flapper of the penguin.
{{right|''A. R. Wallace'', Nat. Selec., p. 24.}}}}

'''antitype''' (an′ti-tīp), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀντίτυπον, neut. of ἀντίτυπος, corresponding, as the stamp to the die, < ἀντί, against, corresponding to, + τύπος, a model, type: see ''type.''] '''1.''' That which is prefigured or represented by a type, and therefore is correlative with it; particularly, in ''theol.'', that which in the gospel is foreshadowed by and answers to some person, character, action, institution, or event in the Old Testament.
{{smaller block|It is this previous design, and this preordained connection (together, of course, with the resemblance), which constitute the relation of type and ''antitype.''
{{right|''Fairbairn'', Typology, I. 46.}}
He [Melchizedek] brought forth bread and wine, . . . imitating the ''antitype'', or the substance, Christ himself.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor.''}}}}
'''2.''' In ''biol.'', same as ''antitrope.''

'''antitypic''' (an-ti-tip′ik), ''a.'' Same as ''antitypical.''
{{smaller block|A series of ''antitypic'' groups. {{right|''Cope.''}}}}

'''antitypical''' (an-ti-tip′i-kal), ''a.'' [< ''antitype'' + ''-ical.'' Cf. ''typical.''] Pertaining to or of the nature of an antitype.
{{smaller block|The writer [of the Epistle to the Hebrews] recognizes the typical, or rather ''antitypical'', character of the Tabernacle and its services, as reflecting the archetype seen by Moses}}

